From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 07:40:11 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] images: say what a download will take, and fetch it through
 luna-downloadmgr

"waydroid init" on a machine without pre-installed images downloads the
system and vendor zips from the OTA channel. On the current channel that is
954 MB for arm64 (912 MB system, 42 MB vendor) and 712 MB for armv7, which
unpack to 2.1 GB and 1.4 GB. On LuneOS this started the moment the Waydroid
icon was tapped, with nothing on screen for as long as it took and no way to
know it was happening, let alone over mobile data.

Three changes, all inert where luna-send is absent.

"waydroid init --plan FILE" works out what init would download and the room
it needs, and writes that as JSON, without downloading or configuring
anything. It runs the same setup_config as init, against a throwaway config
next to FILE: the real waydroid.cfg existing is what the LuneOS units take to
mean Waydroid is set up. The size of each zip comes from the channel; what it
unpacks to comes from the zip's central directory, read with a few HTTP Range
requests rather than the download. The room needed is the worst moment of
the install - one zip next to everything unpacked so far - per filesystem,
since /media/internal and /var are separate mounts.

images.get() asks both channels before fetching either, so the size of the
whole download is known from the start, and reports progress against it:
into /run/waydroid-luneos/download.json on every update, and as a toast in
the notification list. That is a dashboard when LuneOS ships one
(org.webosports.app.waydroid.download, launched here): a window of its
own in the list, which reads the state file and stays current, with one
light banner to say the download started. Without it there are only
toasts, which cannot be updated, only replaced, each popping a banner -
so then a toast is replaced at 0, 25, 50 and 75 per cent rather than on
each of the twenty updates per file downloadmgr sends. Either one, tapped,
relaunches Waydroid to ask where things are. The state file carries the
writer's pid, so a reader can tell a download whose process was killed.

http.download() hands the transfer to luna-downloadmgr when it answers,
into /media/internal/downloads/waydroid - the manager only accepts a
targetDir under /media/internal from an unprivileged caller, which
luna-send is. It holds a wake lock for the transfer and pauses and resumes it
with the network. Measured on mindphone (armv7) with the 23.5 MB armv7
vendor zip: SourceForge's two redirect hops followed, sha256 matching the
channel's id. Its replies drive what happens here:

  completed and completionStatusCode 200      done; the target path is used
  interrupted (11)                            paused; autoResume carries on
                                              under the same ticket
  aborted (12), or completed with any other   failed - a 404 arrives as
  code                                        completed with -5

Progress uses e_amountReceived, the 64-bit count as a string, not
amountReceived, an int32. Each redirect hop reports its own small total
before the file starts, so progress is measured against the channel's size.

Two things are guarded against because downloadmgr outlives whoever asked
for a file. A SIGTERM - stopping the unit that runs init - is turned into an
exception, so the ticket is cancelled on the way out, and init then exits 143
as the signal would have; and before a download
starts, any earlier one into the same directory still running, queued or
interrupted is cancelled, since downloadmgr resumes interrupted downloads by
itself when it starts and would otherwise fetch the best part of a gigabyte
after a reboot for nobody. A transfer that receives nothing for ten minutes
is given up on.

The replies are read with os.read into a buffer of our own. readline() on
the pipe pulled every pending reply into Python's buffer, and the select()
that followed then waited on an empty pipe with the completion already in
hand - a hang the host tests hit as soon as the last update and the
completion arrived together.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS specific]
